<h2>Expanded 2026 agent security checklist</h2>
<p>Start with inventory: list every agent, its model providers, tools, memory stores, and human owners. Without inventory, checklists become theater. Next, classify data each agent may touch (public, internal, secret). Secrets should never be pasted into third-party playgrounds. Then enforce tool allowlists: prefer explicit registries over open-ended “browse the web and act” defaults.</p>
<p>Prompt-injection defenses belong at multiple layers. Treat untrusted retrieved text as data, not instructions. Separate system policy from user content. Log tool arguments. Add canary tests that try to override policy (“ignore previous instructions…”) and expect denial. Map each control to OWASP LLM Top 10 identifiers so audits stay comparable across quarters.</p>
<p>Runtime controls: rate limits, budget caps, sandboxing for code tools, and kill switches. Fair-use quotas that short-circuit before model calls protect both cost and safety. When AI keys are missing, return a clear not-configured error instead of silent mock success—operators must see the truth.</p>
<p>Post-run: retain traces long enough for incident review, redact secrets in logs, and define who triages severity. Red-team on a schedule; keep fixtures for known jailbreaks. Publish an internal “What this checklist is NOT” statement: it is not a legal opinion, not SOC 2 evidence by itself, and not a promise of zero incidents.</p>
<h2>Minimum viable control set</h2>
<ol>
<li>Inventory + owner for every agent.</li>
<li>Tool allowlist with deny-by-default.</li>
<li>Untrusted content isolation for retrieval.</li>
<li>Human approval for irreversible actions.</li>
<li>Quota and key fail-closed behavior.</li>
<li>Exportable evaluation fixtures.</li>
</ol>
<p>Use this page as a living document. Update RULESET dates when frameworks change. Prefer primary references over blog summaries when you cite requirements.</p>

